BRING ON THE FIGHTS
BRING ON THE LOVE

89
00:04:19,342 --> 00:04:20,510
Good morning.

90
00:04:21,427 --> 00:04:23,137
<i>It's time for Ethics class.</i>

91
00:04:23,221 --> 00:04:27,058
<i>Life can only begin when you stop</i>
<i>running away from your past.</i>

92
00:04:27,141 --> 00:04:30,520
<i>You will now reflect</i>
<i>on the mistakes you have made</i>

93
00:04:30,603 --> 00:04:32,981
<i>and discuss them amongst yourselves.</i>

94
00:04:34,357 --> 00:04:36,776
<i>Listen to other people's failures</i>
<i>and regrets,</i>

95
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,239
<i>so you can learn from them</i>
<i>by comparing them to yours.</i>

96
00:04:41,322 --> 00:04:43,324
<i>For today's class, Mimi,</i>

97
00:04:43,408 --> 00:04:45,493
<i>- you will be going first.</i>
- Do I sit there?

98
00:04:47,078 --> 00:04:48,246
Well, thanks for having me.

99
00:04:48,329 --> 00:04:49,998
- Hey, you've got this.
- It's our pleasure.

100
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:54,294
I guess, my family
has always been kind of complicated.

101
00:04:55,169 --> 00:04:56,087
And, well…

102
00:04:57,714 --> 00:05:02,635
Okay, so, I moved around a lot,
so it was hard for me to fit in at school.

103
00:05:03,136 --> 00:05:06,097
Like, in my first and second years
of junior high,

104
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:09,100
I was always getting
into fights and stuff like that.

105
00:05:09,183 --> 00:05:12,603
Sometimes, the teachers wouldn't even
let me come into the classroom,

106
00:05:12,687 --> 00:05:14,439
and so I couldn't keep up
with all the schoolwork.

107
00:05:14,522 --> 00:05:16,107
KAZUMA (26) - SARA (26)

108
00:05:18,109 --> 00:05:21,529
And my home environment
was just really, really bad.

109
00:05:21,612 --> 00:05:23,948
So, I couldn't get along with my mom.

110
00:05:24,032 --> 00:05:27,243
And between me and my third stepdad,

111
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:29,537
things just never got any better.

112
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:30,747
And in ninth grade,

113
00:05:31,622 --> 00:05:35,084
I think it was right before the exams
to get into high school, in the winter,

114
00:05:35,168 --> 00:05:36,294
I ran away from home.

115
00:05:36,919 --> 00:05:39,714
After that, I started hanging out with
older people who were kind of difficult.

116
00:05:39,797 --> 00:05:40,757
TAI-CHAN (22)
HAIRSTYLIST

117
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:44,052
Then I started working at a girls' bar,
still in ninth grade.

118
00:05:44,135 --> 00:05:46,220
I was kind of just living day to day.

119
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:51,225
And I went through
some pretty scary situations.

120
00:05:51,309 --> 00:05:55,855
One time, I even nearly got dragged
into what could have been a sex crime.

121
00:05:55,938 --> 00:05:58,274
There were days
when I honestly had nothing to eat,

122
00:05:58,358 --> 00:06:00,526
and I didn't know if I'd be able to bathe.

123
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:03,738
So, yeah, I lived like that
for two or three months.

124
00:06:03,821 --> 00:06:06,449
And when I didn't know what to do anymore,

125
00:06:06,532 --> 00:06:09,869
that was when I ended up
being placed in a children's home.

126
00:06:09,952 --> 00:06:13,623
So I got put in this children's home,
but I just couldn't fit in.

127
00:06:13,706 --> 00:06:15,708
And my mom would come visit me there,

128
00:06:15,792 --> 00:06:20,129
and she would say things like,
"I want you to come back home so much."

129
00:06:21,506 --> 00:06:24,217
But the thing is, in my heart…

130
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:28,513
…I just didn't want to go back home,
and I also wanted to get away from my mom.

131
00:06:28,596 --> 00:06:32,558
But I also didn't want
to give up on my future, you know?

132
00:06:32,642 --> 00:06:33,768
RUNE (28)
RAPPER/BUSINESS OWNER

133
00:06:33,851 --> 00:06:36,687
I always thought,
if I didn't have a high school diploma,

134
00:06:36,771 --> 00:06:39,107
I wouldn't be able to do
what I wanted in life.

135
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:41,818
Then, it was totally random, but…

136
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:45,863
somebody suggested
that I try going to a cram school.

137
00:06:46,447 --> 00:06:49,742
And I was excited, 'cause I'd never done
an after-school activity,

138
00:06:50,326 --> 00:06:53,121
and I've always liked meeting people
outside my circle.

139
00:06:53,204 --> 00:06:55,123
So I decided to go to the cram school.

140
00:06:55,206 --> 00:06:57,834
And that was the first time

141
00:06:58,418 --> 00:07:02,422
a teacher really understood
that I wasn't ready to listen to anyone,

142
00:07:02,505 --> 00:07:05,383
and they taught me
at a pace that worked for me.

143
00:07:05,466 --> 00:07:07,718
Yeah, this amazing teacher did that.

144
00:07:09,178 --> 00:07:10,763
It was really incredible.

145
00:07:10,847 --> 00:07:12,890
The class was, like, two hours long,

146
00:07:12,974 --> 00:07:15,476
but I actually only ever studied
for ten minutes.

147
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:18,438
- Oh, really?
- But I'd get praise for having studied.

148
00:07:18,521 --> 00:07:19,355
RYOPI (29)
BAR OWNER

149
00:07:19,439 --> 00:07:22,024
This teacher would talk to me
for the rest of the class.

150
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:25,778
Back then, I thought studying
was, like, super uncool.

151
00:07:26,362 --> 00:07:27,196
I get it.

152
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:29,031
I thought listening to people was uncool.

153
00:07:29,115 --> 00:07:32,160
But in the end, if there's anything
you want to do in life,

154
00:07:32,243 --> 00:07:33,953
you have to study for it.

155
00:07:34,036 --> 00:07:36,539
You even have to study
for a driver's license.

156
00:07:37,206 --> 00:07:40,334
I hated anything that tried
to pigeonhole me when I was a kid.

157
00:07:40,418 --> 00:07:41,919
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

158
00:07:42,003 --> 00:07:44,922
They made assumptions about me.
And I mean, usually…

159
00:07:46,007 --> 00:07:49,302
usually they were right,
but sometimes they were wrong, you know?

160
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:51,804
- They'd always assume I'd done something.
- Yeah.

161
00:07:51,888 --> 00:07:53,473
- They stereotyped you.
- Mmm-hmm.

162
00:07:53,556 --> 00:07:55,391
I also experienced that at school,

163
00:07:55,475 --> 00:07:57,435
where people blamed me
for things I didn't do.

164
00:07:57,518 --> 00:07:59,353
- Yeah.
- Like, "I bet it was him again."

165
00:07:59,437 --> 00:08:00,771
Even though it wasn't.

166
00:08:01,272 --> 00:08:04,358
And after all of that,
I ended up punching the principal.

167
00:08:05,401 --> 00:08:06,486
You crossed the line.

168
00:08:06,569 --> 00:08:09,363
Next thing I knew,
I was in the principal's office,

169
00:08:09,447 --> 00:08:10,823
and I was in handcuffs.

170
00:08:10,907 --> 00:08:14,994
And then, I couldn't go to school anymore,
so that's something I really remember.

171
00:08:15,077 --> 00:08:18,581
Like, I wanted to go to school,
but, at the same time, I couldn't.

172
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:20,917
And part of me thought
it would be lame if I went there,

173
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,503
made friends, and just sat there in class,
actually listening.

174
00:08:23,586 --> 00:08:24,712
Yeah, I get what you're saying.

175
00:08:24,795 --> 00:08:27,340
Yeah, so I totally understand
what you're saying.

176
00:08:28,090 --> 00:08:29,759
- I get it too. For sure.
- Yeah.

177
00:08:29,842 --> 00:08:32,637
Like, it felt kind of cool
to be a rebel, you know.

178
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:33,554
We all know.

179
00:08:33,638 --> 00:08:35,765
It made you cool
if you didn't follow the rules.

180
00:08:35,848 --> 00:08:39,310
- I just wanted to do things my way.
- All of us here were yankii, for sure.

181
00:08:39,393 --> 00:08:41,270
- Yeah. Exactly.
- We've all been through that.

182
00:08:41,354 --> 00:08:43,064
I thought it was just me
who thought like that.

183
00:08:43,147 --> 00:08:44,273
- We all did.
- Guess I was wrong.

184
00:08:44,357 --> 00:08:46,567
Now we know how lame we were.

185
00:08:46,651 --> 00:08:48,528
Turns out we were the lame ones.

186
00:08:48,611 --> 00:08:50,321
But that was the first time that

187
00:08:51,155 --> 00:08:53,449
I met someone who actually understood me,

188
00:08:54,158 --> 00:08:55,701
and without that teacher,

189
00:08:55,785 --> 00:08:59,247
I don't think I would've been able to, uh…

190
00:09:00,456 --> 00:09:02,833
Like, I'm still not mature enough,

191
00:09:02,917 --> 00:09:05,962
but without them,
I probably wouldn't have been able

192
00:09:06,045 --> 00:09:09,549
to do even the most basic shit
you're supposed to do as an adult.

193
00:09:12,969 --> 00:09:14,971
- That was really nice.
- Sweet.

194
00:09:16,305 --> 00:09:17,473
- Okay.
- Hmm.

195
00:09:17,557 --> 00:09:20,393
So, we've heard Mimi
share her story during Ethics class.

196
00:09:20,476 --> 00:09:22,687
You know,
I'm kind of similar to all of them.

197
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:24,689
Like, I was a pretty bad kid.

198
00:09:24,772 --> 00:09:26,440
- Is that so?
- Yes.

199
00:09:26,524 --> 00:09:28,067
Is that so?

200
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:30,111
Well, he seems
to have become a pretty decent…

201
00:09:30,194 --> 00:09:31,821
- Yeah, he's great now.
- …reliable person.

202
00:09:31,904 --> 00:09:34,448
Yeah, I think I managed
to straighten myself out,

203
00:09:34,532 --> 00:09:36,492
but my family situation was unusual…

204
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:37,618
Mmm.

205
00:09:37,702 --> 00:09:40,204
…so I'm not sure if I can speak
for these participants here.

206
00:09:40,288 --> 00:09:43,040
Some of my elementary
and junior high school teachers…

207
00:09:43,124 --> 00:09:45,543
- Uh-huh.
- …were really supportive of me.

208
00:09:45,626 --> 00:09:48,838
Even when I dropped out of school,
they were supportive.

209
00:09:48,921 --> 00:09:50,172
Actually, I'd run away from home.

210
00:09:50,256 --> 00:09:52,633
- Oh, dear.
- I was staying at my grandma's place.

211
00:09:52,717 --> 00:09:56,053
And there was this teacher
who drove all the way to my grandma's,

212
00:09:56,137 --> 00:09:58,556
and I'm still
just really grateful for that.

213
00:09:58,639 --> 00:09:59,640
- Wow.
- Amazing.

214
00:09:59,724 --> 00:10:02,351
Kids grow up
in all different kinds of environments,

215
00:10:02,435 --> 00:10:06,063
and sometimes, they're dealing
with so many things on the inside.

216
00:10:06,147 --> 00:10:06,981
That's true.

217
00:10:07,064 --> 00:10:09,525
It's important to understand
their feelings and accept them.

218
00:10:09,609 --> 00:10:12,320
'Cause life isn't easy,
and everybody's different.

219
00:10:12,403 --> 00:10:14,905
So things might be a little bit hard
when you're a kid.

220
00:10:14,989 --> 00:10:17,158
Having teachers like yours and hers

221
00:10:17,241 --> 00:10:19,619
can dramatically change
the course of your life.

222
00:10:19,702 --> 00:10:21,579
- Right.
- Meeting someone like that is important.

223
00:10:21,662 --> 00:10:23,914
- Mimi was saved by that. That's so great.
- Right.

224
00:10:23,998 --> 00:10:26,083
- What about you, Nagano?
- I don't know what to say.

225
00:10:26,167 --> 00:10:28,336
- No?
- I said on live TV recently

226
00:10:28,419 --> 00:10:30,379
that I don't feel like
any teachers helped me.

227
00:10:30,463 --> 00:10:31,922
What?

228
00:10:32,006 --> 00:10:33,215
There must be one.

229
00:10:33,299 --> 00:10:35,843
Then I did my dance,
but it didn't go down well.

230
00:10:35,926 --> 00:10:37,011
I bet it didn't.

231
00:10:43,809 --> 00:10:47,438
<i>The women will now have</i>
<i>a showdown to compete for a date.</i>

232
00:10:47,521 --> 00:10:49,482
- <i>For real?</i>
- <i>It's the girls this time.</i>

